  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Given name, often a middle name, then the family name. English surnames come from trades, places and fathers (Smith, Wood, Johnson); Scottish ones often start Mac or Mc; Welsh ones grew from patronymics (Jones, Davies, Price from ap Rhys). Irish families carry O' and Mc names. In British Sikh families men often carry Singh and women Kaur, as a surname or a middle name; British Bangladeshi women often carry Begum or Khatun, which is why those stay out of the shared family list. Somali families use the father's or grandfather's given name as the surname. Polish surnames change their ending for women (Kowalski, Kowalska), so the list keeps ones that do not. Hong Kong and Chinese British people often carry an English first name alongside their Chinese one.",
